<item>
  <title>Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors</title>
  <link>https://my-math-notes.example.com/posts/linear-algebra/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 





<section id="the-core-concept" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="the-core-concept">The Core Concept</h2>
<p>An <strong>eigenvector</strong> of a square matrix <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?A"> is a non-zero vector <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?v"> that changes at most by a scalar factor when that linear transformation is applied to it. The corresponding scalar factor <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Clambda"> is called the <strong>eigenvalue</strong>.</p>
<p>The defining equation is: <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0AA%20v%20=%20%5Clambda%20v%0A"></p>
</section>
<section id="visualizing-the-transformation" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="visualizing-the-transformation">Visualizing the Transformation</h2>
<p>When a matrix scales a vector without changing its fundamental direction, that vector is an eigenvector. Here is a geometric representation showing an original vector <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?v"> and its transformation <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?Av">:</p>
<div class="quarto-figure quarto-figure-center">
<figure class="figure">
<p><img src="https://my-math-notes.example.com/posts/linear-algebra/eigenvector-viz.svg" class="img-fluid figure-img" width="400"></p>
<figcaption>Eigenvector transformation diagram</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
</section>
